It's also possible to bind the handlers just based on the identity of the method, as opposed to the first param shared_ptr inner type, like this (test case):
```        instance.subscribe(
            [&amp;](const code&amp; ec, mock::all_required, bool a, double b, std::string c)
            {
                called = true;
                result_a = a;
                result_b = b;
                result_c = c;
                promise8.set_value(ec);
                return true;
            });```
The `mock::all_required` is the actual method type and uniquely identifies the handler as a unique signature. For `object_t` params, the method descriptors also support names for correlation (a, b, c, below)...
```struct mock_methods
{
    static constexpr std::tuple methods
    {
        method&lt;"empty_method"&gt;{},
        method&lt;"all_required", bool, double, std::string&gt;{ "a", "b", "c" },
        method&lt;"with_options", std::string, optional&lt;4.2&gt;, optional&lt;true&gt;&gt;{ "a", "b", "c" },
        method&lt;"with_nullify", std::string, nullable&lt;double&gt;, nullable&lt;bool&gt;&gt;{ "a", "b", "c" },
        method&lt;"with_combine", std::string, nullable&lt;bool&gt;, optional&lt;4.2&gt;&gt;{ "a", "b", "c" },
        method&lt;"not_required", nullable&lt;bool&gt;, optional&lt;4.2&gt;&gt;{ "a", "b" }
    };

    // Derive this from above in c++26 using reflection.
    using empty_method = at&lt;0, decltype(methods)&gt;;
    using all_required = at&lt;1, decltype(methods)&gt;;
    using with_options = at&lt;2, decltype(methods)&gt;;
    using with_nullify = at&lt;3, decltype(methods)&gt;;
    using with_combine = at&lt;4, decltype(methods)&gt;;
    using not_required = at&lt;5, decltype(methods)&gt;;
};```

